{"id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsAndDistance#def:pet-ch5-distance","title":"def:pet-ch5-distance","path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsAndDistance.md","line":244,"body":"","kind":"definition","status":"not_started","tex":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsAndDistance.tex#def:pet-ch5-distance","uses":["[[references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsAndDistance#def:pet-ch5-curve-length]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsAndDistance.tex","label":"def:pet-ch5-distance","line":502,"source":"\\begin{definition}[Riemannian distance]\n  \\label{def:pet-ch5-distance}\n\n  \\source{petersen:page-0194}\n  \\uses{def:pet-ch5-curve-length}\n\n  For $p,q\\in M$ let $\\Omega_{p,q}=\\{c:[0,1]\\to M\\mid c\\text{ piecewise }C^\\infty,\\\n  c(0)=p,\\ c(1)=q\\}$ and define the \\emph{distance} $d(p,q)=|pq|=\\inf\\{L(c)\\mid\n  c\\in\\Omega_{p,q}\\}$. Immediately $|pq|=|qp|$ and $|pq|\\le|pr|+|rq|$; that $|pq|=0$\n  only for $p=q$ is established in \\cref{thm:pet-ch5-metric-topology}. When the\n  metric needs to be specified one writes $|pq|_g$.\n\\end{definition}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Definition</div></section>[Riemannian distance] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-6465663a7065742d6368352d64697374616e6365\"></span> <p></p><span class=\"tex-source\" style=\"display:block\">Source: <span class=\"reference\">petersen:page-0194</span></span> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FPetersen%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh05_GeodesicsAndDistance%23def%3Apet-ch5-curve-length\">↗ def:pet-ch5-curve-length</a> <p></p>For <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$p,q\\in M$\">$p,q\\in M$</span> let <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\Omega _{p,q}=\\{ c:[0,1]\\to M\\mid c\\text{ piecewise }C^\\infty , c(0)=p,\\  c(1)=q\\} $\">$\\Omega _{p,q}=\\{ c:[0,1]\\to M\\mid c\\text{ piecewise }C^\\infty , c(0)=p,\\  c(1)=q\\} $</span> and define the <em>distance</em> <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$d(p,q)=|pq|=\\inf \\{ L(c)\\mid c\\in \\Omega _{p,q}\\} $\">$d(p,q)=|pq|=\\inf \\{ L(c)\\mid c\\in \\Omega _{p,q}\\} $</span>. Immediately <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$|pq|=|qp|$\">$|pq|=|qp|$</span> and <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$|pq|\\le |pr|+|rq|$\">$|pq|\\le |pr|+|rq|$</span>; that <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$|pq|=0$\">$|pq|=0$</span> only for <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$p=q$\">$p=q$</span> is established in <a class=\"reference tex-ref\" href=\"#node=references%2FPetersen%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh05_GeodesicsAndDistance%23thm%3Apet-ch5-metric-topology\" data-tex-node=\"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsAndDistance#thm:pet-ch5-metric-topology\" data-tex-anchor=\"tex-label-74686d3a7065742d6368352d6d65747269632d746f706f6c6f6779\">section 3</a>. When the metric needs to be specified one writes <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$|pq|_g$\">$|pq|_g$</span>. ","warnings":[]},"previous":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsAndDistance#prop:pet-ch5-arclength-reparametrization","next":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsAndDistance#def:pet-ch5-metric-balls","relations":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsAndDistance#def:pet-ch5-curve-length","title":"def:pet-ch5-curve-length","label":"def:pet-ch5-curve-length"}],"backlinks":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsAndDistance#def:pet-ch5-metric-balls","title":"def:pet-ch5-metric-balls","label":"def:pet-ch5-metric-balls"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsA